      faq: {
        id: 'faq',
        title: 'Frequently Asked Questions',
        faqs: [
          { q: 'What is an uncensored AI model?', a: 'An uncensored AI model is an open-weight language model whose refusals have been reduced or removed, either by fine-tuning or by editing its weights. The result is rarely total: published tests of well-known modified models still show residual refusals.' },
          { q: 'What is the difference between uncensored and abliterated AI?', a: 'Uncensored describes an outcome, fewer refusals. Abliterated describes a method, editing the weights to suppress refusal behavior. An abliterated model is usually less restricted, but "uncensored" in a model name proves nothing without a measured result.' },
          { q: 'What is the best uncensored LLM?', a: 'There is no single best. For small machines, 4-9B Gemma and Qwen abliterations fit in about 8 GB. For 24 GB GPUs, 24-31B models such as Dolphin Mistral 24B or the Qwen3.8 27B abliteration fit. Check the model card for evidence, license and recent activity, and test it on your own tasks.' },
          { q: 'Are uncensored AI models free?', a: 'The weights are usually free to download, under licenses such as Apache-2.0 or the Llama community license. You still pay for hardware and electricity, and some licenses carry conditions on use and redistribution.' },
          { q: 'Where can I download uncensored models?', a: 'Hugging Face is the main host for weights and model cards, and Ollama distributes many as ready-to-run tags. ModelScope is an alternative host. Abliter.cn and Abliteration.org are indexes that help you discover models but do not host the files.' },
          { q: 'Can I run uncensored AI locally?', a: 'Yes. Download a GGUF file or an Ollama tag and run it in Ollama, LM Studio or llama.cpp. A 7-9B model needs about 8 GB of memory, and a 24-31B model needs roughly 20-28 GB at Q4.' },
          { q: 'What is Heretic?', a: 'Heretic is an open-source tool by Philipp Emanuel Weidmann that automatically searches for settings that remove refusals while changing the model as little as possible. Version 2.0, published on 2026-11-01, made Arbitrary-Rank Ablation its default method.' },
          { q: 'Does abliteration remove all AI safeguards?', a: 'No. It targets refusal behavior and does not guarantee that every refusal disappears. Published tests show residual refusals, and quality can decrease, particularly on truthfulness benchmarks in the original research.' },
          { q: 'Are uncensored models dangerous?', a: 'They can be misused, and the risk grows when a model is connected to tools such as a shell, files or the web. In a plain chat window it only generates text. Use sandboxing, least privilege, human approval and logging for agents.' },
          { q: 'Is it legal to use uncensored AI in Europe?', a: 'It depends on possession, distribution and use. Owning a model is different from using it to commit an offense. The EU AI Act mainly obliges providers, and licenses such as Llama\'s add contractual limits. This is not legal advice; consult a lawyer for your case.' },
          { q: 'Which local apps can use uncensored models?', a: 'Any app that lets you choose the model, which includes LM Studio, Open WebUI, SillyTavern, AnythingLLM, Msty, KoboldCpp, Cline and n8n through Ollama or OpenAI-compatible connections. Check that the version you use accepts custom models.' },
        ],
      },
